:"The font attribute group gathers together attributes for determining the font within a credit or direction. They are based on the text styles for Cascading Style Sheets. The font-family is a comma-separated list of font names.The font-style can be normal or italic. The font-size can be one of the CSS sizes or a numeric point size. The font-weight can be normal or bold. The default is application-dependent, but is a text font vs. a music font.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"name":"color","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The color attribute group indicates the color of an element.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"name":"halign","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"In cases where text extends over more than one line, horizontal alignment and justify values can be different. The most typical case is for credits, such as:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"name":"valign","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The valign attribute is used to indicate vertical alignment to the top, middle, bottom, or baseline of the text. Defaults are implementation-dependent.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLValign":{"title":"MXLValign","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlvalign","abstract":[{"text":"The valign type is used to indicate vertical alignment to the top, middle, bottom, or baseline of the text. If","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the text is on multiple lines, baseline alignment refers to the baseline of the lowest line of text. Defaults","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"are implementation-dependent.","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLValign"}],"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLValign"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLValign"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony/Chord-swift.struct/Kind-swift.struct":{"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony\/chord-swift.struct\/kind-swift.struct","title":"MXLHarmony.Chord.Kind","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Kind","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Kind","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Kind indicates the type of chord. Degree elements can then add, subtract, or alter from these starting points."}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony\/Chord-swift.struct\/Kind-swift.struct"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony":{"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLHarmony","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"MXLHarmony","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLHarmony","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"The harmony type represents harmony analysis, including chord symbols in popular music as well as functional","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"harmony analysis in classical music.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony","role":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLColor":{"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlcolor","title":"MXLColor","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLColor"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLColor"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The color type indicates the color of an element. Color may be represented as hexadecimal RGB triples, as in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"HTML, or as hexadecimal ARGB tuples, with the A indicating alpha of transparency. An alpha value of 00 is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"totally transparent; FF is totally opaque. If RGB is used, the A value is assumed to be FF."}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLColor"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLLeftCenterRight":{"title":"MXLLeftCenterRight","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlleftcenterright","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLLeftCenterRight","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLLeftCenterRight","abstract":[{"text":"The left-center-right type is used to define horizontal alignment and text justification.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLLeftCenterRight","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony/Chord-swift.struct":{"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Chord","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"MXLHarmony.Chord","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Chord","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"A harmony element can contain many stacked chords (e.g. V of II). A sequence of harmony-chord groups is used","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"for this type of secondary function, where V of II would be represented by a harmony-chord with a 5 numeral"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"followed by a harmony-chord with a 2 numeral."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony\/chord-swift.struct","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony\/Chord-swift.struct","role":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony/Chord-swift.struct/Kind-swift.struct/init(value:usesSymbols:text:areDegreesStacked:areDe